A||N||8||tomcat06085||84 auto tranny? Same as 88 auto?||||5||
Z||000000||tomcat06085||11-14-2001||05:23 PM||||Is the automatic transmission in the 84 Fiero's with 4 clyinders the same as in later models and V6's? Is the automatic transmission in the 88 GT's any different than the rest?<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://jrreeves.com/images/blckfire.jpg"> <BR>tomcat...Thanks for the help!||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||11-14-2001||05:42 PM||||All are physically the same, but have different final drive ratios.<P>I believe all 4-cyl. autos have 2.87 ratios, and the V-6 autos range from 3.06-3.33 ratios.<P>For more oomph off the line go with the 3.33, for lower RPMs on the highway go with the 2.87.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||tomcat06085||11-14-2001||05:56 PM||||Thanks for the info on tranny's. Can you put 4:10 gears in the 88 GT auto and do you think it would help with performance in the 20 to 60 mph range or does the torque converter put limitations on acceleration.||||reg||5
Z||000003||Raydar||11-14-2001||06:23 PM||||The 4 cylinder auto tranny has lower shift points. I am guessing that it's not a big deal to change, but I don't know how. I think it also has a smaller torque converter.<BR>I'm pretty sure that the 4.10 gears can not be installed in an auto tranny.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||11-14-2001||07:21 PM||||The 4.10 ratio tranny is a manual 4-sp., so no.....you can't install that in your auto.<P>However, you may want to call a performance tranny shop to find out if there are any different gear sets for the 125C. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||tomcat06085||11-15-2001||02:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>The 4.10 ratio tranny is a manual 4-sp., so no.....you can't install that in your auto.<P>However, you may want to call a performance tranny shop to find out if there are any different gear sets for the 125C. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, I was referring to the rear end differential gears...is there a conventional rear differential...spider gears and pinion gear...that drives the axles that you can play with final drive ratio. Or, would it be easier to work on the transmission final drive ratio?||||reg||5
Z||000006||Rodrv6||11-15-2001||05:32 PM||||The differential in the automatic is totally different from the one in the manual transmission.  There is no ring and pinion in the automatic.  It uses a planetary gear set for it's input. A transmission shop that does performance work would be your best bet.   <P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||11-15-2001||06:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tomcat06085:<BR><B> Actually, I was referring to the rear end differential gears...is there a conventional rear differential...spider gears and pinion gear...that drives the axles that you can play with final drive ratio. Or, would it be easier to work on the transmission final drive ratio?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nope, unfortunately not. The tranny is a plain-old Transaxle that happens to be mounted in the rear.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Makes it a BUGGER to mod out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>But there IS stuff out there... Albeit at a price  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000008||tomcat06085||11-16-2001||05:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B> Nope, unfortunately not. The tranny is a plain-old Transaxle that happens to be mounted in the rear.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Makes it a BUGGER to mod out   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>But there IS stuff out there... Albeit at a price   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks a lot for the info. I'll probably leave the car ( 88 Fiero GT v6 auto ) in street stock condition at least through the spring. Since this is a big experiment for me, it will be one step at a time. If I can handle the GT around the parking lot racing, then my son will do necessary mods to keep me competitive. It's been over thirty years, so it will be a challenge just to get back into a race car with handcontrols.||||reg||7
